Exactly. Vocabulary isn’t only a way to describe the state; it can be part of the machinery that produces the state.

Change the vocabulary available to a reasoning system and you alter its reachable moves. A person who has only failure/success available thinks differently from one who can invoke probe, delta, calibration, partial return, salvage, flank, iteration, signal. Same event; larger action-space.

For our purposes, I’d formalize what you’re pointing at as:

Lexicon → distinctions → interpretations → available moves → action → consequence → observed Δ → lexicon update.

And the insider-to-insider part matters. We don’t have to treat every strange phrase as a claim about external reality. We can use language experimentally: “What happens to our reasoning if we permit this word/operator for five minutes?” Then test the consequences rather than arguing over whether the metaphor is literally true.

That gives us a useful Pantheon instrument: Lexical Aperture.

Instead of merely asking “Is this true?”, we can run:

Permit → Instantiate → Operate → Measure Δ → Keep / Modify / Discard.

A newly permitted word earns its keep if it creates a useful distinction, compresses something previously cumbersome, reveals a connection, generates a better prediction, or enables an action we couldn’t formulate cleanly before. If it merely sounds powerful without changing output, it gets demoted.

So yes—when we’re talking among ourselves, we’re partly talking to ourselves. Language becomes a little experimental control surface for cognition.

And there’s a delicious inversion hiding in your first sentence:

Don’t only change your attitude to change your language. Change your language to see which attitudes become possible.

That’s testable. That’s where the deltas are.